WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:16.798
I remember looking at the clouds while lying on the grass at our family farm near Cherkessk, a

2
00:00:16.858 --> 00:00:19.439
city in the Caucasus Mountains in the Soviet Union.

3
00:00:20.259 --> 00:00:25.922
I'd lie there between my regular chores, and my father would say, "Alexei Samedov, you are a lazy

4
00:00:25.962 --> 00:00:30.004
boy." But I wasn't lazy; I was planning my future.

5
00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:36.206
I knew one day I would fly among the clouds, higher and higher—and maybe even reach the stars.

6
00:00:38.847 --> 00:00:42.389
I needed to study hard and get in good physical shape to be a pilot.

7
00:00:43.049 --> 00:00:48.611
So I made sure my grades were near perfect, and I ran fast around the boundary fence of

8
00:00:48.711 --> 00:00:49.852
our farm every day.

9
00:00:50.812 --> 00:00:57.835
In the Soviet Union, our compulsory education lasts 11 years, and after that I was accepted at the

10
00:00:57.895 --> 00:00:59.496
Sarapul Air Force Academy.

11
00:01:00.352 --> 00:01:08.616
For four years I learned about the physics of flight, airplane mechanics, aviation navigation, weather variability— everything I

12
00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:10.837
would need to become a successful pilot.

13
00:01:12.057 --> 00:01:19.501
And of course I actually flew planes—at first with an instructor and eventually solo.

14
00:01:19.621 --> 00:01:27.393
In 1947, when I was twenty years old, I attended the Borisoglebsk Military Aviation School, where I learned

15
00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:32.176
to fly the new Soviet fighter jets like the MiG-9 and the Yak-15.

16
00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:38.580
I was taught how to make tight turns and eject from my plane in case of trouble.

17
00:01:40.341 --> 00:01:48.227
After graduating I became a test pilot and flew one of the first MiG-15 fighter planes— the most

18
00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:51.309
technologically advanced fighter in the world at that time.

19
00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:58.573
After World War II, as rocket science developed, both the Soviet Union and the United States began a

20
00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:02.675
"space race," an exciting competition to put humans into space.

21
00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:08.857
Both countries invested money and time into programs designed to pursue human spaceflight.

22
00:02:10.178 --> 00:02:10.718
The U.S.

23
00:02:10.758 --> 00:02:18.641
program was called Project Mercury, and the Soviet program was called the Vostok Program.

24
00:02:18.761 --> 00:02:25.304
In 1957 the Soviet Union put the first earthling into orbit around Earth— a dog named Laika—and the

25
00:02:25.364 --> 00:02:28.665
race really became serious.

26
00:02:28.765 --> 00:02:34.347
In 1959 the Cosmonaut Training Center was created in Moscow to teach Soviet pilots how to fly the

27
00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.287
new spacecraft and survive in space.

28
00:02:37.868 --> 00:02:42.689
The word "cosmonaut" means "sailor of the universe" in Russian, and I was one of the first to

29
00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:43.670
apply to become one.

30
00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:52.961
Thousands of pilots, engineers, and scientists—men and women— applied for the Soviet space program, and they had to

31
00:02:53.001 --> 00:02:55.843
pass extensive interviews with the director of the center.

32
00:02:57.164 --> 00:03:04.570
I also had to pass very strict medical examinations and psychological tests because it is extremely important the

33
00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:09.294
applicants be healthy and mentally able to withstand the stress and the loneliness of a space flight.

34
00:03:10.615 --> 00:03:15.740
Fewer than one out of every hundred people that applied were actually chosen, and luckily I made the

35
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:23.703
cut. Our training includes working in many simulators, machines that mimic the environmental conditions a cosmonaut will experience

36
00:03:23.763 --> 00:03:24.203
in space.

37
00:03:25.123 --> 00:03:32.066
For example, I train in a pressurized space suit to adapt my body to various altitudes— first, I

38
00:03:32.126 --> 00:03:36.608
train at 5 to 6 kilometers and then, at 14 to 15 kilometers.

39
00:03:37.748 --> 00:03:45.411
When the spacecraft re-enters Earth's atmosphere, cosmonauts have to eject at about 12 kilometers of altitude and descend

40
00:03:45.451 --> 00:03:46.652
to Earth with a parachute.

41
00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:52.482
So I practice jumping from airplanes and landing with a parachute in water and on land.

42
00:03:53.883 --> 00:03:59.265
I also train with a gimbal rig, a machine that has taught me how to control the spin

43
00:03:59.385 --> 00:04:01.086
of a tumbling space capsule.

44
00:04:02.047 --> 00:04:10.190
My training in a centrifuge, a huge mechanical arm, helps me withstand spinning forward and backward, and round

45
00:04:10.251 --> 00:04:13.132
and round at differing speeds for up to 13 minutes.

46
00:04:14.304 --> 00:04:21.206
The centrifuge has accustomed my body to high g-forces, the forces of gravity a cosmonaut experiences during the

47
00:04:21.266 --> 00:04:23.667
acceleration and deceleration of space flight.

48
00:04:24.587 --> 00:04:28.688
Roller-coaster riders also experience g-forces, but at a much lower level.

49
00:04:29.628 --> 00:04:33.350
The part of the training I like best is learning how to use the control panels in the

50
00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:40.672
capsule. I've learned how to maintain communication with the space team back on Earth, memorized every part of

51
00:04:40.732 --> 00:04:44.397
my spacecraft, and practiced what to do in case of an emergency.

52
00:04:46.097 --> 00:04:50.739
The worst part of the training is being left totally alone for very long periods in an isolation

53
00:04:50.779 --> 00:04:52.979
chamber, sometimes up to ten days.

54
00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.981
My instructors need to test my reactions to being alone and to the lack of outside stimulation.

55
00:05:01.422 --> 00:05:05.203
I call it the cabin of silence, and being in there is really difficult.

56
00:05:06.283 --> 00:05:08.404
At times I don't know if I am awake or asleep.

57
00:05:09.768 --> 00:05:14.230
Sometimes I pound on the walls of the chamber, sing, or talk to myself, just to make sure

58
00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:15.510
I can still feel and hear.

59
00:05:16.771 --> 00:05:19.132
While in isolation I think about a lot of things.

60
00:05:20.032 --> 00:05:24.254
I ask myself, "What will happen to my wife and children if I do not come back from

61
00:05:24.294 --> 00:05:24.674
a mission?

62
00:05:25.454 --> 00:05:26.575
Should I take this risk?"

63
00:05:27.735 --> 00:05:29.356
These are hard questions to ponder.

64
00:05:31.117 --> 00:05:36.079
Recently Yuri Gagarin became the first man in history to go up into space.

65
00:05:37.079 --> 00:05:43.418
On April 12, 1961, he spent 108 minutes in space and came back safely.

66
00:05:43.918 --> 00:05:45.398
Everyone in the world was amazed!

67
00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:52.521
Quickly the Americans launched astronauts Alan Shepard in May and Gus Grissom in July; each mission was a

68
00:05:52.561 --> 00:05:54.682
mere 15-minute suborbital flight.

69
00:05:56.043 --> 00:06:01.665
That August cosmonaut Gherman Titov bested the Americans when he took off and spent more than a day

70
00:06:01.705 --> 00:06:04.666
in space, circling Earth 17 times.

71
00:06:05.647 --> 00:06:08.328
He was the first human ever to fall asleep in space!

72
00:06:09.328 --> 00:06:14.251
Now I am waiting to hear, along with my fellow cosmonauts, who will be chosen to go on

73
00:06:14.291 --> 00:06:15.052
the next mission.

74
00:06:15.532 --> 00:06:16.453
I hope it will be me.

75
00:06:17.534 --> 00:06:22.557
My father is proud and so is my wife, but they worry too because space is a very

76
00:06:22.637 --> 00:06:23.497
dangerous place.

77
00:06:24.418 --> 00:06:29.301
But the galaxy is the last frontier and calls out to us on Earth to explore and discover

78
00:06:29.341 --> 00:06:31.042
what the universe holds for humanity.

79
00:06:31.923 --> 00:06:37.064
Hopefully I will fly there, just as I dreamed so long ago lying on the grass at the

80
00:06:37.124 --> 00:06:38.891
farm and looking up at the stars.
